## Page Pattern

```razor
@page "/alerts/{Id:guid}"
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = Policies.OperatorOrAbove)]
@inject IAlertApiClient AlertClient
@inject ISnackbar Snackbar

<PageTitle>Alert @Id</PageTitle>

<MudContainer MaxWidth="MaxWidth.Large">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h4">Alert Detail</MudText>
    @if (_loading)
    {
        <MudProgressCircular Indeterminate="true" />
    }
    else if (_alert is null)
    {
        <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Warning">Alert not found.</MudAlert>
    }
    else
    {
        <AlertSummaryCard Alert="_alert" />
    }
</MudContainer>

@code {
    [Parameter] public Guid Id { get; set; }

    private AlertDetailDto? _alert;
    private bool _loading = true;

    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        _loading = true;
        try
        {
            _alert = await AlertClient.GetByIdAsync(Id);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Snackbar.Add($"Failed to load alert: {ex.Message}", Severity.Error);
        }
        finally
        {
            _loading = false;
        }
    }
}
```

## Authorization

- Every page has `[Authorize(Policy = ...)]` at the top.
- Use policies from `SecureOps.Shared.Auth.Policies`, not raw role names.
- Hide UI elements with `<AuthorizeView Policy="...">`.
- Server enforces — UI hiding is a courtesy, never a security boundary.

```razor
<AuthorizeView Policy="@Policies.AdminOnly">
    <Authorized>
        <MudButton OnClick="OpenAdminPanel">Admin</MudButton>
    </Authorized>
</AuthorizeView>
```

## State Management

- Scoped services for per-user state (`AddScoped<TService>`).
- No `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` — Blazor Server holds state on the server.
- For cross-component coordination, use a scoped mediator service or a Blazor `CascadingValue`.
- Avoid singletons holding user-specific data.

## Forbidden Patterns

| Pattern | Reason |
|---|---|
| `<form>` HTML with full page submit | Use Blazor `EditForm` |
| Direct DbContext injection in `.razor` files | UI calls API, not DB |
| Direct PowerShell invocation in UI | UI calls API; Worker runs PowerShell |
| `JSInterop` for fundamental UI behavior | Use Blazor components |
| Inline styles for theming | Use MudBlazor theme |
| Custom CSS that conflicts with MudBlazor | Extend MudBlazor theme instead |

## Theme

- Define theme in `Program.cs` via `MudThemeProvider`.
- Color palette: navy primary, amber accent, neutral grays. See `docs/03-architecture.md` for tokens.
- Dark mode optional; default light.

## Real-Time Updates

Use SignalR via Blazor's built-in hub (no separate SignalR setup needed in Blazor Server).

For alert list page:
- On page open, subscribe to a server-side observable.
- Worker writes new alerts to DB and publishes a domain event.
- UI scoped service subscribes and triggers `StateHasChanged()` when new alerts arrive.

## Forms and Validation

Use `EditForm` with `DataAnnotationsValidator` or `FluentValidation` validators.

```razor
<EditForm Model="_model" OnValidSubmit="SubmitAsync">
    <DataAnnotationsValidator />
    <ValidationSummary />

    <MudTextField @bind-Value="_model.Name" Label="Server Name" Required="true" />
    <MudButton ButtonType="ButtonType.Submit" Color="Color.Primary">Submit</MudButton>
</EditForm>
```

## Accessibility

- All interactive components have keyboard navigation.
- Color is never the only signal — pair color with icon or label.
- ARIA attributes on custom components.
- Test with screen reader for primary flows in Phase 2.

## "Audit Is Not Surveillance" Framing

When building any UI element that shows audit data:

- Page title: "Operational Audit", not "User Activity".
- Filter labels: "Action type", "Time window", not "Operator name first".
- Default sort: by time, not by user.
- No leaderboards. No "fastest responder" widgets. No comparison-of-operators charts.

If a feature concept conflicts with this framing, surface it for ADR review.

